 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 520268,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "In an experiment, researchers grew plant seedlings in soils to which one of two strains of bacteria were added. A control group had no bacteria added to the soil. The seedlings’ uptake of the nutrient potassium increased dramatically in the soil with Strain 1 and decreased dramatically in the soil with Strain 2. Based on these findings, draw both specific and broad inferences about the relationship between the bacteria, the seedlings, and available nutrients.",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1016523,
     "content_html": "The Strain 2 bacteria increased the availability of potassium in the soil, and this nutrient was needed and used by the seedlings in the soil. The Strain 1 bacteria decreased the availability of potassium in the soil."
    },
    {
     "id": 1016524,
     "content_html": "The soil with Strain 1 bacteria must have had more potassium in comparison to soil with Strain 2 bacteria. The seedlings took up more potassium in Soil 1 than in 2 due to this difference."
    },
    {
     "id": 1016525,
     "content_html": "The Strain 1 bacteria increased the availability of potassium in the soil, and this nutrient was needed and used by the seedlings in the soil. The Strain 2 bacteria decreased the availability of potassium in the soil."
    },
    {
     "id": 1016526,
     "content_html": "The Strain 1 bacteria decreased the availability of potassium in the soil,  and this nutrient was needed and used by the seedlings in the soil. The Strain 2 bacteria increased the availability of potassium in the soil."
    }
